
# THE COPY OF THIS FILE MUST BE MODE 0600. This template carries no secrets and is
# committed world-readable, which is right for a template and wrong for the .env made from
# it: that one holds the PostgreSQL password, the Kafka cluster-superuser credential, the
# event publisher's credential and the sample subscriber's, and "cp" reproduces this file's
# 0644 so every account on the host can read all four.
#
# './stack.sh --init' handles it: it creates .env with mode 0600 as the file comes into
# existence, repairs the mode of an .env that already exists BEFORE writing anything into
# it, generates every secret above, and fills in only the keys that are still missing - so
# it is safe to re-run against an environment already in service, and it is how an .env
# written before Kafka existed gains its Kafka keys.
#
# If you copy this file by hand instead, run 'install -m 600 .env.example .env' rather than
# 'cp', and set every empty credential yourself.

# Which docker-compose file to use by default
COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yaml

# Command line for Compose. LEAVE IT EMPTY AND stack.sh DETECTS IT.
#
# COMPOSE 2.20.0 OR NEWER IS REQUIRED, and it is not a preference. The compose files declare
# "depends_on: <service>: required: false" on the Kafka dependency, which is what makes the
# broker opt-in rather than a hard dependency of the default bring-up. Compose added that field
# in 2.20.0, so an older release cannot parse the files at all.
#
# The standalone "docker-compose" script is Compose v1. It is end-of-life and it is BELOW that
# floor, so it is no longer detected or accepted: this key was once pinned to it, and on a
# current Docker "./stack.sh --up" then failed with "docker-compose: command not found" before it
# reached the stack. Empty means "use the Compose plugin on this host, after checking its
# version"; set it explicitly only to name a wrapper, a remote context or an absolute path, and
# note that stack.sh version-checks whatever it is given.
#COMPOSE_CL="docker compose"
COMPOSE_CL=

# docker-compose stack name
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=blnk

# Blnk project name
BLNK_PROJECT_NAME=Blnk

# Timezone
TZ=Etc/UTC

# Server details
BLNK_SERVER_SSL_DOMAIN=
BLNK_SERVER_SSL_EMAIL=
BLNK_SERVER_SSL=
BLNK_SERVER_PORT=

# Which proxies may be believed when they say who the client is.
#
# A comma-separated list of CIDR blocks or bare IP literals. LEAVE IT EMPTY UNLESS BLNK
# SITS BEHIND A PROXY YOU OPERATE: empty means trust nothing, and the client address in
# the access log is then the peer that actually opened the connection, which a caller
# cannot forge.
#
# Set it to the addresses of your own load balancer or ingress if you need the real client
# address through it - Blnk will then believe X-Forwarded-For and X-Real-IP, but only when
# the request arrived from one of these addresses.
#
# DO NOT set it to 0.0.0.0/0. That tells Blnk to believe the forwarded header on every
# request, which lets any caller choose the address recorded against their requests.
#
# A malformed entry stops the API from starting rather than being ignored, because
# silently falling back would leave the forgeable behaviour in place while this file said
# otherwise.
#
# IT IS ALSO THE SECOND HALF OF BLNK_SERVER_TRUST_FORWARDED_PROTO BELOW. That flag declares
# that a proxy owns X-Forwarded-Proto — the header that decides whether the one-time SASL
# password from POST /subscribers/{id}/kafka-credentials may travel on this connection — and
# this list is what says WHICH peers that declaration covers. A declaration with nothing
# named here, or with a universal range, is treated as no declaration at all, because a
# matcher that matches every caller is what the declaration was introduced to replace.
#BLNK_SERVER_TRUSTED_PROXIES=10.0.0.0/8,192.168.1.10
BLNK_SERVER_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# Boolean, DEFAULT FALSE. Declares that a fronting proxy terminates TLS and that this
# deployment's X-Forwarded-Proto may therefore be BELIEVED.
#
# It exists for one decision: POST /subscribers/{id}/kafka-credentials returns a SASL
# password, and the response carrying it is the only chance to disclose that password to
# anything watching the wire, so Blnk serves it only over a channel it can establish as
# confidential. It accepts three, and they are not equally strong: this process terminating
# TLS is PROVEN from the connection; loopback is READ from the socket; and a fronting proxy
# is only ever DECLARED — by this variable.
#
# Deny-by-default is deliberate. With no in-process TLS and no declaration, issuance refuses
# with SUBSCRIBER_INSECURE_TRANSPORT rather than putting a password on a channel nobody has
# established as confidential. That includes a loopback caller, which needs the separate
# declaration below.
#
# SETTING THIS WITHOUT SUCH A PROXY IS UNSAFE, and it fails silently. X-Forwarded-Proto is a
# header any client can set, so enabling this without a proxy in front lets a caller assert
# its own confidentiality and receive a password over plaintext. Two conditions must hold
# before you set it:
#
#   1. The proxy must SET AND OVERWRITE X-Forwarded-Proto on every request — never append to
#      a client-supplied value, or a forged entry survives alongside the real one.
#   2. No path may reach the process or pod DIRECTLY. Any route that bypasses the proxy
#      arrives with whatever header the client chose, which is precisely the disclosure the
#      default prevents.
#
# BLNK_SERVER_TRUSTED_PROXIES BELOW IS REQUIRED WITH THIS FLAG, and it is what turns
# condition 2 from an operational promise into something the process checks. The header is
# believed only on a request whose SOCKET PEER is one of the addresses that variable names,
# so a caller that reaches this process by another route — a pod IP, a port-forward, a second
# Service, an ingress rule that passes the client's header through — is refused however it
# sets the header. With this flag on and that list empty or set to a universal range
# (0.0.0.0/0, ::/0), the forwarded channel establishes NOTHING and issuance refuses with
# SUBSCRIBER_INSECURE_TRANSPORT; with BLNK_SERVER_SECURE=true that combination is refused at
# start-up instead of running in a state that looks configured.
#
# Nothing else in Blnk's behaviour depends on it.
#BLNK_SERVER_TRUST_FORWARDED_PROTO=true
BLNK_SERVER_TRUST_FORWARDED_PROTO=false

# Boolean, DEFAULT FALSE. LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY. Declares that no proxy sits in front of
# this process, so a request whose PEER is a loopback address may be answered with a Kafka
# credential over plaintext.
#
# Why this is a declaration rather than something Blnk can work out: a loopback peer proves
# only that the LAST hop stayed on this host. A reverse proxy on the same host — nginx,
# Caddy, an Envoy sidecar — accepts a request from the internet over plain http and forwards
# it over 127.0.0.1, so from inside the process that request is indistinguishable from an
# operator running curl in the container. Believing the peer would then disclose a one-time
# SASL password on the public hop. Only you know which shape this host is.
#
# The peer is read from the accepted SOCKET and never from X-Forwarded-For, so a remote
# caller cannot present itself as local by setting a header.
#
# DO NOT SET THIS IN PRODUCTION, including on a single host with a proxy in front of Blnk.
# Use BLNK_SERVER_SSL, or terminate TLS at a proxy and declare it with
# BLNK_SERVER_TRUST_FORWARDED_PROTO above. Nothing else in Blnk's behaviour depends on it.
#BLNK_SERVER_ALLOW_LOOPBACK_CREDENTIAL_ISSUANCE=true
BLNK_SERVER_ALLOW_LOOPBACK_CREDENTIAL_ISSUANCE=false

# Per-client request rate limit
#
# THESE ARE PER CLIENT ADDRESS, not per deployment. Leave them empty to accept the defaults:
# 2000 requests per second with a burst of 4000. Both halves must be present for the limiter
# to run at all - the middleware disables it entirely if either is missing - so setting only
# one of the two turns rate limiting OFF rather than half on.
#
# WHY THE DEFAULT IS 2000 AND NOT SOMETHING TIGHTER. The shipped default used to be 5,000,000
# per second with a burst of 10,000,000, which no client can reach, so the limiter never
# engaged and the control existed on paper only. 2000 is four times the 500 events/second this
# project's own acceptance criterion states, so it cannot throttle a deployment running at the
# volume Blnk is specified for, while still bounding a single abusive caller.
#
# READ THIS WITH BLNK_SERVER_TRUSTED_PROXIES ABOVE. Until that names your proxy, every request
# keys to the proxy's address, so a per-client limit behaves as a GLOBAL one and a tight value
# throttles your whole deployment rather than one caller. Set the trusted proxies first, then
# tighten this.
BLNK_RATE_LIMIT_RPS=
BLNK_RATE_LIMIT_BURST=
BLNK_RATE_LIMIT_CLEANUP_INTERVAL_SEC=

# Search index credential
#
# The api-key your TypeSense deployment was started with. REQUIRED when BLNK_SERVER_SECURE is
# true and BLNK_TYPESENSE_DNS names a host: the configuration is refused rather than falling
# back, because the built-in fallback is the literal "blnk-api-key" published in this
# repository's compose files, so it is known to everyone and grants full access to the
# collection holding indexed transaction, balance and identity records.
#
# Leave it empty for a local stack (secure mode off), where that literal is exactly what the
# bundled TypeSense is started with and is doing no security work. Leave it empty in production
# too if you do not use search - with no BLNK_TYPESENSE_DNS configured nothing is indexed, and
# Blnk starts normally after saying so once.
BLNK_TYPESENSE_KEY=

# DNS details
BLNK_DATA_SOURCE_DNS=
BLNK_REDIS_DNS=

# Log verbosity
# One of trace, debug, info, warn, error, fatal or panic, matched case-insensitively.
# Read by Blnk itself, from this variable or from "log_level" in blnk.json, and applied
# to every log line both roles emit. Leave it unset for info, which is the shipped
# default and the right production level.
#
# WARNING IS THE QUIETEST LEVEL BLNK ACTUALLY RUNS AT. error, fatal and panic are
# accepted and then RAISED to warning, and the override is announced in the log naming
# both the level you asked for and the level in force. The reason is directly below:
# every failed publish attempt must be recorded, those records are warnings, and logrus
# discards warnings at any of those three levels - so they would not have made Blnk
# quieter, they would have deleted the only evidence of why an event was retried or
# dead-lettered. Everything from warning upwards behaves exactly as you set it, and
# warning still suppresses the per-event info and debug lines that are almost all of
# the pipeline's volume.
#
# SET IT TO debug TO INVESTIGATE EVENT DELIVERY. Several of the event pipeline's
# diagnostics are per-event and are therefore emitted at debug on purpose - the
# successful-publish line in the relay and in the publisher, the metrics collector's
# per-tick summary, and the notice that a consumer-lag series has been retired because
# its subscriber is gone. At the 500 events per second this pipeline is built for, a
# line per published event saying "it worked" is volume rather than observability, so
# they stay off by default; this is how you turn them on without recompiling. Failure
# logging is NOT affected - every failed publish attempt is logged at warning with its
# attempt number and error reason, and because warning is the floor described above,
# that record survives every value this variable will accept.
#
# debug is verbose in proportion to throughput. It is a diagnostic setting for the
# duration of an investigation, not a deployment default.
#
# A value this list does not contain is reported as a warning and the current level is
# kept, so a typo here cannot stop the process starting.
BLNK_LOG_LEVEL=

# Postgres credentials
#POSTGRES_IMAGE=postgres:latest
POSTGRES_IMAGE=postgres:alpine3.19 #alphine version is smaller in size
POSTGRES_CONTAINER=pg_server
POSTGRES_OUTER_PORT=5432
POSTGRES_INNER_PORT=5432
POSTGRES_USER=postgres
POSTGRES_PASSWORD={POSTGRES_PASSWORD}
POSTGRES_DB=blnk
POSTGRES_SCHEMA=blnk

# Application image
# Read by docker-compose.yaml only, for BOTH the server and the worker so the two process
# roles cannot come from different builds. Blnk itself never reads it.
#
# IT HAS NO DEFAULT ON PURPOSE. docker-compose.yaml used to fall back to a published
# release, which meant `docker compose up` quietly ran a build older than your checkout —
# no Kafka publishing, no relay, no /events, no subscriber registry, and no error saying
# so. Left unset now, starting the server or the worker fails with "pull access denied for
# set-blnk-image-explicitly-see-env-example", which names the problem instead of hiding it.
# Interpolation still succeeds either way, so `docker compose config` and the
# infrastructure-only commands keep working with this empty.
#
# Pin it by digest, not by tag: a tag can be repushed over a different image, a digest
# cannot. Example:
#
#     BLNK_IMAGE=jerryenebeli/blnk@sha256:<digest of the release you tested>
#
# To run the code in this working tree instead of a release, use docker-compose.dev.yaml,
# which builds from source and needs none of this.
BLNK_IMAGE=

# Redis
# REDIS_PASSWORD is read by docker-compose.yaml and docker-compose.dev.yaml, which pass it
# to redis-server as --requirepass when it is non-empty and start Redis unauthenticated
# when it is empty. Blnk does NOT read this key; it reads the password out of the DSN, so
# setting one without the other is the one mistake to avoid here.
#
# SET BOTH TOGETHER. internal/redis-db's ParseRedisURL takes the password from the DSN's
# userinfo, so a password on the server needs the matching form in BLNK_REDIS_DNS above:
#
#     REDIS_PASSWORD=<password>
#     BLNK_REDIS_DNS=redis://:<password>@localhost:6379      # host processes
#     BLNK_REDIS_DNS=redis://:<password>@redis:6379          # inside compose
#
# Use rediss:// instead of redis:// wherever the connection crosses a host boundary; the
# parser negotiates TLS from the scheme.
#
# EMPTY IS THE SHIPPED DEFAULT AND IS SAFE ONLY BECAUSE OF THE LOOPBACK BIND. The compose
# port mapping is 127.0.0.1 by default, and the 37 Go test files that dial
# localhost:6379 as a literal — plus the redis service in .github/workflows/go.yml — depend
# on the unauthenticated default, which is why it is opt-in rather than mandatory.
# SETTING REDIS_OUTER_HOST TO ANYTHING OTHER THAN 127.0.0.1 WITHOUT SETTING REDIS_PASSWORD
# PUBLISHES AN UNAUTHENTICATED REDIS ON THE NETWORK. Redis holds TRANSACTION_QUEUE, so that
# is write access to the ledger's work queue.
REDIS_PASSWORD=
REDIS_OUTER_HOST=127.0.0.1
REDIS_OUTER_PORT=6379

# Kafka event streaming
# Read by Blnk itself. Every key in this block mirrors a field of config.KafkaConfig,
# in declaration order, so the block and the struct can be diffed by eye. Blnk reads
# them from its process environment, or from the matching keys under "kafka" in
# blnk.json.
#
# FOUR OF THEM ARE THE DEPLOYMENT CONTRACT: KAFKA_BROKERS, KAFKA_TOPIC_PREFIX,
# KAFKA_SASL_ADMIN_USER and KAFKA_SASL_ADMIN_SECRET. Together with the three RELAY_*
# keys further down and WEBHOOK_DEPRECATION_SUNSET_DATE they are the eight variables
# requirement R-10 fixes, and their names, defaults and meanings do not change.
#
# Everything else in this block is a SUPPLEMENTARY setting - Blnk's own, revisable, and
# never required for a working deployment because each has a safe default or a
# documented fallback. Each is labelled where it appears, with the reason it exists:
# KAFKA_SASL_USER/SECRET for producer least privilege, KAFKA_SASL_SECRET_FILE as the
# no-print delivery channel for a generated one, KAFKA_TLS_* for transport security,
# KAFKA_INSECURE_LOCAL_DEV as the explicit local-dev acknowledgement,
# KAFKA_MIN_PARTITIONS/KAFKA_REPLICATION_FACTOR for topic geometry,
# KAFKA_HISTORICAL_TOPIC_PREFIXES so a namespace rename does not strand the events
# captured before it, and KAFKA_ALLOW_PARTITION_GROWTH as consent for a
# reordering-unsafe operation. The
# distinction is recorded here rather than left to a document because the two have
# different standing, and confusing them is how a contract quietly grows.
#
# The missing BLNK_ prefix is deliberate - do not "correct" it. envconfig builds each
# field's primary key by accumulating the prefix through every enclosing struct and
# consults the raw tag literal only as an alternate key, so for this nested block the
# prefixed primary envconfig derives is BLNK_KAFKA_KAFKA_BROKERS.
#
# The ordinary BLNK_-prefixed alias of every key in this block - BLNK_KAFKA_BROKERS,
# BLNK_KAFKA_TOPIC_PREFIX and so on - ALSO resolves, and wins when both forms are set.
# It is applied explicitly by applyPrefixedEnvAliases in config/config.go, because
# envconfig alone reads it under neither key: a deployment that set it used to get
# default behaviour with no error to explain why nothing was published. Either
# spelling below is therefore supported; the bare one is the mandated contract.
#
# Leaving KAFKA_BROKERS empty is a legitimate steady state, not an error: with no
# brokers configured the event publisher resolves to its no-op implementation and
# Blnk starts, serves and processes transactions exactly as it did before Kafka
# existed. Set it only when you want events published. Comma-separated; whitespace
# around each entry is trimmed and empty entries are dropped. Use the broker's
# service name inside the compose network, or localhost:9092 for a host-run binary.
#
# SETTING IT OBLIGES YOU TO SET WEBHOOK_DEPRECATION_SUNSET_DATE in the same change.
# Configuring a broker starts the dual-delivery window, and a window with no stated end
# is refused: the load FAILS rather than starting a deployment whose retirement date
# nobody has chosen. See that key below for the reasoning and for how to generate the
# instant.
#
# IN THE COMPOSE STACK THIS IS ONLY HALF OF OPTING IN. The kafka and kafka-init
# services sit behind the "kafka" Compose profile, so setting this key alone leaves
# the relay retrying against a broker nobody started. Set COMPOSE_PROFILES=kafka near
# the bottom of this file as well, or pass --profile kafka.
#KAFKA_BROKERS=kafka:9092
KAFKA_BROKERS=

# The SUBSCRIBER-FACING bootstrap list: the externally advertised broker addresses
# POST /subscribers/{id}/kafka-credentials reports to a subscriber. It is a DIFFERENT
# LIST from KAFKA_BROKERS above, not an alias for it.
#
# KAFKA_BROKERS is what Blnk itself dials, and inside a deployment that is an internal
# address - "kafka:9092" here, a ClusterIP or headless Service in Kubernetes. Neither
# resolves for a subscriber outside the deployment, and Kafka compounds it: a broker
# answers every client with the ADVERTISED address of the listener the connection
# arrived on, so even a reachable bootstrap hands back internal names for the partition
# leaders. Only an operator knows the externally advertised list.
#
# IT IS REQUIRED FOR CREDENTIAL ISSUANCE. With this empty, POST
# /subscribers/{id}/kafka-credentials answers 503 SUBSCRIBER_BROKERS_NOT_CONFIGURED naming this
# variable, and no credential is created. It does NOT fall back to KAFKA_BROKERS.
#
# The fallback existed, with a warning on every issuance, and the warning was not a
# control: it landed in Blnk's log while the consequence landed on the subscriber. An
# internal address reported to an outside subscriber surfaces as an unexplained
# connection timeout in THEIR logs days later, and because the SASL secret is shown
# exactly once, diagnosing it costs a reissue. Refusing is the cheaper failure - it is
# immediate, it names the variable to set, and it discloses no internal address.
#
# Every other endpoint runs without this. Only issuance requires it, and only because
# only an operator knows the externally advertised list. A deployment whose subscribers
# really do run inside it sets this to the same value as KAFKA_BROKERS, which makes that
# claim explicit in one line rather than implicit in a fallback.
#
# The local value is the broker's EXTERNAL listener, which docker-compose.yaml publishes
# on the host and advertises as ${KAFKA_OUTER_ADVERTISED_HOST:-localhost}:9092. A
# deployment whose subscribers really do run inside it sets this to the same value as
# KAFKA_BROKERS, which makes that claim explicit in one line.
KAFKA_SUBSCRIBER_BROKERS=localhost:9092

# Whether anything in front of the brokers AUTHORISES RECORD KEYS. This is what decides
# whether a subscriber recording a partition_key_prefix can be issued a credential at all.
#
#   none           (default) Nothing authorises keys. Issuance for a prefix-recording
#                  subscriber is REFUSED with 409 SUBSCRIBER_KEY_SCOPE_UNENFORCED, before any
#                  secret is generated and before any ACL is bound.
#   broker_gateway A component in front of the brokers evaluates record keys. Key-scoped
#                  issuance is permitted, and the credential names that component.
#
# WHY A REFUSAL AND NOT A DISCLOSURE. Kafka's authorizer evaluates cluster, topic, group,
# transactional id, delegation token and user resources. A message key is none of them, so
# NO ACL narrows a principal to a slice of a topic's records. Issuing anyway and reporting
# partition_key_prefix_enforced=false hands out unrestricted access to a shared stream and
# relies on the integrator reading a flag; an unread flag is not a control.
#
# An unrecognised value is read as "none" with a warning at configuration load. The failure
# direction for an authorization control is closed.
KAFKA_KEY_SCOPE_ENFORCEMENT=none

# The addresses that key-authorising component listens on, comma-separated. Reported to a
# subscriber as its "brokers" IN PLACE OF KAFKA_SUBSCRIBER_BROKERS, so the credential names
# the endpoint that actually enforces its declared boundary.
#
# ONLY HONOURED WITH KAFKA_KEY_SCOPE_ENFORCEMENT=broker_gateway, and only when this list is
# non-empty AND DIFFERENT from KAFKA_BROKERS. A gateway list equal to the brokers cannot be
# evaluating keys - it IS the brokers - so that combination is read as no declaration at
# all, warned about at start-up, and issuance refuses as if the mode were "none".
#
# BLNK DOES NOT SHIP THE GATEWAY. It validates this declaration, routes credentials to it
# and reports it. Whatever terminates these addresses is responsible for rejecting records
# outside a principal's prefix. Declaring the mode without operating such a component
# produces credentials claiming an enforcement point that does not enforce, which is worse
# than the refusal. Leave both keys at their defaults until the component is real.
KAFKA_KEY_SCOPE_GATEWAY_BROKERS=

# The CONTROL ENDPOINT of that component, and the only part of the declaration above Blnk
# can verify rather than take on trust.
#
# The mode and the gateway addresses are assertions this deployment makes about itself, and
# any address satisfies them. So without a usable endpoint here the declaration is treated
# as NO declaration: a key-scoped subscriber is REFUSED rather than issued a credential
# whose response announces an enforced key boundary while its ACLs deliberately withhold
# topic Read on the strength of a component nobody checked was there.
#
# WHAT BLNK DOES WITH IT, which is the whole contract this endpoint has to implement:
#
#   POST <url>     Bind a key scope. Body: {"principal", "subscriber_id",
#                  "partition_key_prefix", "authorized_topics", "consumer_group_prefix"}.
#                  Must answer 200 with {"key_scope_enforced": true, "principal": <the same
#                  principal>, "partition_key_prefix": <the same prefix, byte for byte>}.
#                  Sent during credential issuance, BEFORE any secret exists and before the
#                  broker is touched; an answer that differs in either field, or that is not
#                  200, refuses the issuance with 409 SUBSCRIBER_KEY_SCOPE_UNATTESTED.
#   DELETE <url>?principal=<p>  Withdraw a binding. Sent during deregistration, after the
#                  broker credential is revoked. 404 counts as success. A failure here does
#                  NOT fail the deregistration - no access remains once the principal cannot
#                  authenticate - it is logged at WARNING as a stale entry to clean up.
#   GET <url>      Health. Must answer 200 with {"key_scope_enforced": true}. Probed once at
#                  server start-up and logged; it never blocks start-up.
#
# Plain http is accepted only for a loopback host. Anything else must be https: the token
# below registers key-scope bindings, so whoever holds it decides which records a subscriber
# sees. Redirects are refused rather than followed, because following one forwards the token.
KAFKA_KEY_SCOPE_GATEWAY_ATTESTATION_URL=

# The bearer credential presented on those calls. A SECRET - keep it out of source control,
# exactly like the SASL credentials above. Required for the declaration to be usable at all.
KAFKA_KEY_SCOPE_GATEWAY_ATTESTATION_TOKEN=

# How long that component has to answer, in milliseconds. Default 2000, and the application
# clamps anything above 5000 down to it: credential issuance answers within five seconds, so
# a longer budget here could only turn a refusal into a timeout on the endpoint itself.
KAFKA_KEY_SCOPE_GATEWAY_ATTESTATION_TIMEOUT_MS=2000

# THE OTHER DECLARATION A DEPLOYMENT CAN MAKE about its subscriber access model, and with
# BLNK_SERVER_SECURE=true it must make one of the two.
#
# Set true to acknowledge that a subscriber credential reads EVERY record on each topic it
# is granted - every ledger's, and every other subscriber's - because Kafka authorizes
# topics and consumer groups and has no message-key dimension.
#
# THIS IS NOT A DEFECT AND SAYING SO IS NOT A WORKAROUND. It is the access model the design
# mandates: category topics, no per-tenant topics. For a single-tenant ledger or a trusted
# internal consumer it is exactly right. What was wrong is that it used to be the model a
# deployment arrived at by configuring NOTHING - the widest credential Blnk can issue, handed
# out because nobody had considered tenancy. Documented is not decided.
#
# So in secure mode, with neither this nor KAFKA_KEY_SCOPE_ENFORCEMENT=broker_gateway
# declared, issuance refuses with 409 SUBSCRIBER_SHARED_TOPIC_ACCESS_UNACKNOWLEDGED. Outside
# secure mode nothing changes, which is what keeps the local stack working untouched.
KAFKA_SUBSCRIBER_SHARED_TOPIC_ACCESS=false

# THE INTERNAL-TOPIC ACKNOWLEDGEMENT. Default: false, and false is the safe answer.
#
# Set true to acknowledge that a subscriber granted <KAFKA_TOPIC_PREFIX>.system reads Blnk's
# OWN error text verbatim - a PostgreSQL error naming schema, table, column and routine; a
# broker error naming internal addresses - for the whole deployment, plus every event type
# the catalogue does not yet recognise, because that topic is also the catch-all.
#
# WHY THE VARIABLE EXISTS AT ALL. system.error was delivered to webhook subscribers, so
# retiring the HTTP transport without a credential-reachable equivalent would drop that
# audience outright. Its payload is frozen field-for-field by the event schema, so the
# disclosure cannot be narrowed by redaction. Only an operator knows whether this
# deployment's subscribers are internal consumers who may see its internals or third parties
# who may not, so the decision is recorded here rather than guessed in code.
#
# WHAT IT CHANGES. One thing: whether <prefix>.system may appear in a subscriber's
# authorized_topics. With it false the request DTO, the persistence boundary and the ACL
# provisioner all refuse that name and say so, naming this variable. With it true the name
# becomes grantable and a subscriber still receives it only by listing it explicitly -
# nothing derives it, no default includes it, and no wildcard reaches it. It grants nothing
# on its own and does not affect publishing: the internal topic is created and written to
# either way. Unlike the shared-topic acknowledgement above it is read in EVERY mode, not
# only secure mode.
#
# ledger.created does NOT need this: it is on blnk.ledgers, an ordinary tenant topic.
KAFKA_SUBSCRIBER_INTERNAL_TOPIC_ACCESS=false

# The prefix every topic name is derived from. Default: blnk, which yields the five
# category topics blnk.transactions, blnk.balances, blnk.identities, blnk.ledgers and
# blnk.system, plus their blnk.<category>.dlt dead-letter siblings - ten owned topics in
# total. FOUR of those category topics are grantable to subscribers, per subscriber:
# blnk.transactions, blnk.balances, blnk.identities and blnk.ledgers. blnk.system is not in
# that default set - it carries system.error, whose frozen payload includes verbatim internal
# error text, and it is the catch-all for any event type the catalogue does not recognise, so
# it is an operator topic and is grantable only where KAFKA_SUBSCRIBER_INTERNAL_TOPIC_ACCESS
# is set. No dead-letter topic is ever grantable, whatever is declared. See
# docs/event-streaming.md.
KAFKA_TOPIC_PREFIX=blnk

# The topic namespaces this deployment USED TO own and must still be able to publish to.
# Comma-separated, and EMPTY unless you have changed KAFKA_TOPIC_PREFIX with events still
# in the outbox - which is almost every deployment.
#
# WHY RENAMING THE PREFIX NEEDS THIS. An outbox row records its fully-resolved destination
# topic at INSERT time, so a committed event stays bound to the topic it was always meant
# for. Change KAFKA_TOPIC_PREFIX from blnk to acme and the rows already in the table still
# name blnk.transactions, while the publisher, topic assurance and the ownership test all
# now speak of acme.transactions. The RUNNING process survived that - it had pre-created a
# writer for every blnk.* topic at start-up. The next RESTART did not: it pre-created acme.*
# only, and every stored blnk.* row was then refused at writer resolution. Nothing was lost,
# and every attempt logged the refusal, but nothing drained those rows either, and the same
# refusal stranded the dead-letter write of a failing row and the replay of a dead-lettered
# one. A rename plus a rolling restart is ordinary maintenance, and it stopped delivery of
# everything captured before it.
#
# WHAT LISTING A PREFIX DOES. Every name here is treated as owned exactly as
# KAFKA_TOPIC_PREFIX is: writers are pre-created for its whole topic inventory, the
# ownership test admits it, topic assurance keeps its topics present, and its topic names are
# reported verbatim in metrics instead of collapsing to the "unowned" label. It does NOT
# change where NEW events go - KAFKA_TOPIC_PREFIX alone decides that.
#
# THE LIST IS MEANT TO BE DRAINED, not accumulated. Remove a prefix once no non-terminal and
# no replayable row still names it. The server audits the outbox at start-up and reports, by
# name, any namespace its rows still name and this deployment no longer owns - so if you
# rename and forget this variable, the boot log tells you which value to add and how many
# events are waiting behind it. At most four prefixes may be declared: each one pre-creates a
# writer per topic on every process start.
#
# It is an explicit allowlist rather than "accept any prefix with a known category" on
# purpose. The looser rule would also admit attacker.transactions, which has exactly the same
# shape as a real topic name, and writer resolution is the one place a stored string turns
# into an outbound connection carrying Blnk's own producer credentials.
#KAFKA_HISTORICAL_TOPIC_PREFIXES=blnk
KAFKA_HISTORICAL_TOPIC_PREFIXES=


# SASL/SCRAM principal the STEADY-STATE EVENT PUBLISHER authenticates as - the
# identity the SERVER process uses to write events. It needs only Write
# and Describe on the topics Blnk owns, and it must NOT be the administrative
# principal below.
#
# GIVE IT TO THE SERVER ONLY, for the same reason the admin pair below is server-only.
# Only the server role publishes: blnk.ProcessRole.PublishesEvents() is true for the
# server and false for the worker, so the worker resolves to the no-op publisher and
# never opens a producer connection. What the worker does is capture event rows into
# blnk.event_outbox inside the ledger transaction, which is a PostgreSQL write and needs
# no broker credential at all. The compose worker service therefore receives only
# KAFKA_BROKERS and KAFKA_TOPIC_PREFIX and no SASL pair of either kind.
#
# REQUIRED once KAFKA_BROKERS is non-empty. This used to be advisory: leaving it empty
# made the publisher fall back to the administrative credential with a warning. The
# default was backwards, because a deployment reaches that fallback by leaving two
# variables unset - the state every deployment starts in - so a warning was the only
# thing standing between an ordinary rollout and publishing every ledger event as the
# principal that can create topics, mint SCRAM credentials and rewrite ACLs. A warning
# also changes nothing about what a leaked credential can then do.
#
# The publisher now REFUSES to start in that state and names these two variables. See
# KAFKA_ALLOW_ADMIN_PRODUCER below for the deliberate, temporary escape hatch.
#
# scripts/kafka-provision.sh creates this principal and its Write/Describe grants for
# the local stack, from KAFKA_PRODUCER_USER / KAFKA_PRODUCER_SECRET below; the compose
# services fall back to those two names so one .env entry serves both provisioning and
# runtime. stack.sh --init generates them.
#
# TWO NAMING RULES ARE ENFORCED AT START-UP, AND A VIOLATION IS FATAL ONCE
# KAFKA_BROKERS IS SET:
#
#   1. This name must NOT begin with 'blnk-sub-'. That namespace is reserved for the
#      principals Blnk DERIVES for subscribers, and issuing credentials performs a SCRAM
#      upsert - so a producer identity inside it could be made to have its own
#      credential rotated and returned in an API response body.
#   2. It must be DIFFERENT from KAFKA_SASL_ADMIN_USER. The two exist to separate
#      publishing from administration: this one holds Write on the Blnk-owned topics,
#      that one can mint subscriber credentials and rewrite ACLs.
#
# Comparison is case-sensitive, because Kafka principals are.
#KAFKA_SASL_USER=blnk-producer
KAFKA_SASL_USER=

# Never commit a real secret here; see the note on KAFKA_SASL_ADMIN_SECRET below,
# which applies verbatim.
#
# In the compose stack you normally leave this and KAFKA_SASL_USER above EMPTY and set
# the KAFKA_PRODUCER_* pair below instead: the compose SERVER service defaults these two
# from those two, so one generated value both mints the principal on the broker and is
# presented by the publishing process, and the two cannot drift. Set these names
# explicitly only to point at a principal provisioned outside this stack.
KAFKA_SASL_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local-stack principal provisioning
#
# The two blocks below are read by scripts/kafka-provision.sh, not by Blnk. They say
# which principals the local broker should hold; the KAFKA_SASL_* keys above say which
# one Blnk presents.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The STEADY-STATE PRODUCER principal scripts/kafka-provision.sh creates, and the pair
# the compose SERVER service defaults KAFKA_SASL_USER / KAFKA_SASL_SECRET from.
#
# Its grant is Write and Describe on every Blnk-owned topic — each category topic and
# each .dlt sibling — and nothing else: no Read, no consumer group, no cluster
# operation. The dead-letter topics are included because dead-lettering is
# a publish: an event that exhausts its retry budget is written to <topic>.dlt by the
# same process on the same connection.
#
# ./stack.sh --init generates the secret into a mode-0600 .env. Left empty, the
# principal is SKIPPED rather than generated with a printed password — a generated
# password has to be delivered somewhere, and kafka-init's stdout is a container log
# that would keep it indefinitely.
KAFKA_PRODUCER_USER=blnk-producer
KAFKA_PRODUCER_SECRET=

# The SAMPLE SUBSCRIBER principal, a local-development convenience: nothing in Blnk
# needs it, and with the secret empty it is skipped and says so.
#
# Its grant is Read and Describe on GRANTABLE category topics only — never a .dlt
# sibling, which is the one exclusion the allowlist makes unconditionally — plus Read on
# consumer groups under its own prefix. That is the same default allowlist
# model.SubscriberGrantableTopics enforces for a real subscriber, so a grant this script
# will not make is one POST /subscribers/{id}/kafka-credentials would also refuse by
# default.
#
# FOUR categories are grantable by default — transactions, balances, identities and
# ledgers — and this sample DEFAULTS to all four. blnk.system is not: it carries
# system.error's verbatim internal error text and every uncatalogued event, so naming it in
# KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_TOPICS is refused. The API can grant it, on a deployment that has
# set KAFKA_SUBSCRIBER_INTERNAL_TOPIC_ACCESS=true and whose subscriber grant names it; THIS
# SCRIPT never does, whatever that variable says, because a bring-up script makes no
# entitlement decision.
#
# KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_TOPICS NARROWS that grant to a comma-separated subset. It
# cannot widen it: every entry is checked against the same allowlist.
#
# KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_GROUP_PREFIX IS DERIVED, WHICH IS WHY IT SHIPS EMPTY. The
# consumer-group namespace is "<principal>." - the principal above with the delimiter
# model.CanonicalConsumerGroupNamespace appends - and the script builds it rather than
# reading it, because a PREFIXED group grant reaches as far as whoever names it decides.
# Left empty it follows the principal, so renaming KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_USER cannot
# leave a stale namespace behind. A value may still be supplied as a cross-check and is
# accepted with or without the trailing '.', but any other namespace stops provisioning.
#
# Real subscriber credentials are never minted by a script. They are issued by
# POST /subscribers/{id}/kafka-credentials, which returns the secret once and persists
# only a non-reversible reference to it.
KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_USER=blnk-sample-subscriber
KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_SECRET=
# LEAVE THIS EMPTY. The namespace is derived, and the grant is a PREFIXED pattern, so an
# unterminated prefix reaches further than it reads: it would also match
# "blnk-sample-subscriber-evil". Rename the principal and the namespace follows it.
KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_GROUP_PREFIX=
KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_TOPICS=

# LAST-RESORT COMPATIBILITY SWITCH. Leave it false.
#
# Setting it true permits the event publisher to authenticate with the ADMINISTRATIVE
# credentials when no producer principal is configured. It exists for exactly one
# case: an existing single-credential deployment that must keep publishing while it
# provisions a producer principal, rather than stop dead on an upgrade. Every
# publisher construction that takes this path logs a security warning naming it.
#
# Running on it means the process handling the highest request volume in the
# deployment holds unconditional authority over the cluster's access model. Provision
# the producer principal and clear this.
KAFKA_ALLOW_ADMIN_PRODUCER=false

# SASL/SCRAM administrative principal, used for topic assurance, subscriber SCRAM
# credential provisioning, ACL grants and revocation, and the offset reads behind the
# consumer-lag metrics. Empty by default and never defaulted in code, because it is a
# credential: an unconfigured admin principal simply means no provisioning is
# attempted. The local stack bootstraps "admin".
#
# GIVE IT TO THE SERVER ONLY. cmd/server.go is the one process that builds a Kafka
# admin client; the worker publishes and consumes asynq queues and never does, so the
# compose worker service deliberately does not receive this pair. Putting the
# strongest identity in the cluster into a second process buys nothing it can use and
# doubles the surface it can be taken from. Publishing is KAFKA_SASL_USER's job.
#
# THE SAME TWO NAMING RULES APPLY, and they matter more here because this is the
# strongest identity in the cluster: it must NOT begin with 'blnk-sub-', and it must
# differ from KAFKA_SASL_USER. A name inside the reserved namespace is reachable by an
# ordinary authorized API call - register a subscriber whose identifier derives it, call
# the credential endpoint, and the response returns a working superuser password.
# Start-up refuses such a configuration once KAFKA_BROKERS is set, and the write path
# refuses again immediately before the SCRAM upsert.
#KAFKA_SASL_ADMIN_USER=admin
KAFKA_SASL_ADMIN_USER=

# Never commit a real secret here. This ships empty rather than as a brace
# placeholder like the POSTGRES_PASSWORD one above, and deliberately so: stack.sh
# --init substitutes that one name and nothing else, so any other placeholder would
# survive into .env as a literal password. Note that its sed is global, which is why
# no comment in this file spells a placeholder out.
#
# './stack.sh --init' fills this key in anyway, through a different route that reads
# the empty assignment rather than a placeholder - and it does so for an .env that
# already exists as well as for one it creates, so an environment written before
# Kafka existed gains the key by re-running it. For anything else, inject the value
# from your secret store, or set it by hand for local work - together with the
# username above, never on its own.
KAFKA_SASL_ADMIN_SECRET=

# Transport security. BOTH Kafka clients - the event publisher and the administrative
# client - refuse to dial unless one of the two switches below is set, and shipping
# with neither set is deliberate: a deployment must CHOOSE, in writing, between
# verified TLS and an explicitly acknowledged plaintext connection. Neither can be
# arrived at by leaving a variable unset.
#
# Ledger events carry financial amounts and identity events carry names, email
# addresses, phone numbers, addresses and dates of birth, so an unencrypted broker
# connection exposes exactly the data this system exists to protect. SASL/SCRAM does
# not help here: it authenticates without a plaintext password crossing the wire, but
# it does not encrypt the messages that follow.
#
# PRODUCTION: set KAFKA_TLS_ENABLED=true and point KAFKA_TLS_CA_FILE at the PEM
# bundle that signed the brokers' certificates. Add KAFKA_TLS_CERT_FILE and
# KAFKA_TLS_KEY_FILE together for mutual TLS. Set KAFKA_TLS_SERVER_NAME when brokers
# are reached through an address that does not match their advertised name.
#KAFKA_TLS_ENABLED=true
#KAFKA_TLS_CA_FILE=/etc/blnk/kafka/ca.pem
#KAFKA_TLS_CERT_FILE=/etc/blnk/kafka/client.pem
#KAFKA_TLS_KEY_FILE=/etc/blnk/kafka/client-key.pem
#KAFKA_TLS_SERVER_NAME=kafka.internal.example.com
KAFKA_TLS_ENABLED=false

# Last resort only, and warned about on every configuration load: TLS still encrypts
# but no longer AUTHENTICATES, so an interposed broker is indistinguishable from the
# real one. Configure KAFKA_TLS_CA_FILE instead.
KAFKA_TLS_INSECURE_SKIP_VERIFY=false

# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY. Permits an unencrypted connection, which is what the local
# single-broker KRaft stack in docker-compose listens for (SASL_PLAINTEXT). It is the
# only reason plaintext is reachable at all, and it is warned about on every
# configuration load so its presence in a real deployment cannot go unnoticed.
KAFKA_INSECURE_LOCAL_DEV=true

# Permits the topic-assurance pass to raise the partition count of a topic that
# ALREADY HOLDS MESSAGES. Leave it false.
#
# Growing a live topic re-maps keys to partitions - a key hashed into partition 2 of
# six lands somewhere else out of twelve - so one aggregate's history is split across
# two partitions and its events can be consumed out of order. That breaks the
# per-aggregate ordering guarantee irreversibly for every key already written.
# Assurance therefore refuses and reports instead, unless an operator has planned the
# migration and set this. An EMPTY topic is always grown: with no records written
# there is no mapping to preserve.
KAFKA_ALLOW_PARTITION_GROWTH=false


# Permits provisioning to continue when the broker CANNOT SAY whether a principal
# already holds a SCRAM-SHA-512 credential. Leave it false.
#
# Preserving an existing credential depends on being able to ask whether one exists.
# When that question cannot be answered - a broker mid-restart, a timeout, an
# administrative principal without DescribeConfigs on user entities - the answer is
# "unknown", and unknown is NOT absent. Treating it as absent sends the principal into
# the generate-and-upsert path, replacing a working password under no rotation flag and
# breaking every consumer and publishing process holding the current one, while the
# provisioning run reports success.
#
# So an indeterminate probe SKIPS THAT PRINCIPAL rather than aborting the run. No
# credential is written, the principal is left exactly as it is, its disposition is
# reported as `indeterminate` in the summary, and its ACLs are STILL asserted because ACL
# addition is idempotent and cannot invalidate an existing credential. Topic assurance and
# every other principal continue, so the run can still finish successfully.
#
# Setting this to true inverts that: the run then treats an unknown state as ABSENT and may
# REPLACE a credential that already exists, which stops every process holding the current
# password from authenticating — and nothing in the run can tell you whether it happened.
# Set it only for a broker that can never answer a describe on users; the log then says
# plainly what may be overwritten.
# Rotation itself stays explicit, through KAFKA_ROTATE_PRODUCER_SECRET or
# KAFKA_ROTATE_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_SECRET, each needing somewhere to deliver the result.
KAFKA_ALLOW_SCRAM_PROBE_FAILURE=false


# Minimum partitions per topic; 6 is the required minimum. Topics are grown to this
# count when they already exist and are never shrunk. Messages are keyed by ledger
# ID, so ordering is per aggregate regardless of the count - more partitions buy
# parallelism, they do not weaken the ordering guarantee.
KAFKA_MIN_PARTITIONS=6

# 1 locally, 3 in production. A single-broker KRaft cluster cannot satisfy a
# replication factor of 3 - topic creation fails outright - so the local stack must
# say 1. The shipped code default is 3 for production durability, and because
# defaults only fill unset values the explicit 1 below always wins.
KAFKA_REPLICATION_FACTOR=1

# Webhook deprecation
# The dual-delivery window: Kafka publishing and legacy HTTP webhook delivery run
# concurrently from the same outbox rows for exactly 30 days, which is what guarantees
# both transports carry an identical payload. From the sunset instant onwards Kafka is
# the only transport and the deprecated webhook management routes answer 410 Gone.
# Both behaviours read one decision point, event_sunset.go, so they can never disagree
# about the date.
#
# THERE IS ONE SETTING, and it is the sunset. The window's opening instant is DERIVED
# as sunset minus 30 days and has no variable of its own, so "exactly 30 days" is
# arithmetic rather than something you have to get right. An earlier revision of this
# file also accepted WEBHOOK_DEPRECATION_START_DATE; that name is retired and is now
# IGNORED if set, in both its bare and BLNK_-prefixed forms.
#
# A MALFORMED VALUE IS FATAL. Stating a retirement instant and mis-typing it would
# otherwise resolve silently to "the sunset has not passed", so one typo would keep the
# deprecated HTTP transport running indefinitely with nothing failing anywhere.
#
# IT IS REQUIRED WHENEVER KAFKA_BROKERS IS SET, and leaving it empty there is FATAL:
# the configuration is refused and the process does not start. That is not a stricter
# reading of an optional key, it is the only reading that matches the runtime. A
# publishing deployment with no window has nothing to honour, and the sunset predicate
# fails closed on one - it answers "already passed", which stops dual delivery and
# retires the webhook management routes. Accepting the combination with a warning would
# therefore promise continued dual delivery while the running service did the opposite,
# and the disagreement would be silent, because nothing fails when a transport quietly
# stops. Refusing to load says it once, before any traffic is served.
#
# So the two keys are set TOGETHER. If you are turning Kafka on, compute the instant in
# the same change; see the generator commands below.
#
# WITH NO BROKERS IT STAYS OPTIONAL and empty is the norm - there is no transport to
# migrate to, so there is no window to describe, and nothing about a Kafka-less
# deployment changes. That is why the key can ship empty here at all: this template also
# ships KAFKA_BROKERS empty, which is the one combination in which an empty sunset is
# legal.
#
# THIS KEY SHIPS EMPTY, and that is a decision rather than an omission: a date committed
# in a template is in the past shortly after it is written, and a window that has already
# closed is worse than an absent one because it silently ends dual delivery. './stack.sh
# --init' does not fill it in either, for the same reason - an initialised stack is
# brought up and torn down repeatedly, so any instant it chose would be arbitrary.
#
# SET IT TO A DATE 30 DAYS FROM YOUR OWN CUTOVER, computed when you deploy. No example
# value is given on purpose: a literal date in a template is wrong the moment it is
# copied, and a sunset already in the past switches the legacy transport off
# immediately - which for a deployment mid-migration means subscribers that have not
# moved yet stop receiving anything. Generate it instead, for example:
#
#   date -u -d '+30 days' +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ     # GNU date
#   date -u -v+30d +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ            # BSD/macOS date
#
# Being a top-level field it answers to BLNK_WEBHOOK_DEPRECATION_SUNSET_DATE as well,
# the prefixed form winning if both are set.
WEBHOOK_DEPRECATION_SUNSET_DATE=

# Legacy webhook destination policy (SSRF-01)
# The legacy HTTP transport requires https and refuses to deliver to an address inside
# Blnk's own network. That is not a check on the URL you typed: the address is judged
# immediately before the socket is opened, which is the only place a hostname that
# resolves to 127.0.0.1 is visible, and every redirect is refused outright because a
# 307 or 308 from a subscriber endpoint would repost your signed ledger payload
# wherever it pointed.
#
# Set this to true ONLY when the destination is on a network you own — an on-premise
# receiver behind RFC1918, or a loopback sink in development. It opens exactly two
# things: http, and delivery to loopback and private/ULA addresses.
#
# It is NOT an off switch, and it cannot be used as one. With it set to true these
# stay refused: every redirect, the link-local range including the 169.254.169.254
# cloud metadata endpoint, the unspecified address, multicast, the carrier-grade NAT
# range (100.64.0.0/10, where the GKE metadata proxy lives), the benchmarking and
# IETF protocol-assignment ranges, an internal IPv4 address smuggled through the
# NAT64 well-known prefix, and every non-HTTP scheme. Enabling it logs a warning
# naming what it permitted, so the assertion is visible in the deployment's log.
#
# Default false. Leave it unset in production unless you deliver internally.
#BLNK_WEBHOOK_ALLOW_PRIVATE_DESTINATION=true
BLNK_WEBHOOK_ALLOW_PRIVATE_DESTINATION=false

# Event relay retry
# Bounded exponential backoff for the outbox-to-Kafka relay, mirroring
# config.RelayConfig field for field - all three are R-10 CONTRACT VARIABLES with the
# defaults the requirement fixes - and read from the process environment or the
# matching keys under "relay" in blnk.json. The relay makes at most
# RELAY_MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS attempts, waits RELAY_RETRY_BASE_BACKOFF_MS before the
# first retry and doubles thereafter, and never sleeps longer than
# RELAY_RETRY_MAX_BACKOFF_MS. Both delays are milliseconds and are used verbatim.
#
# The defaults below give five publish attempts separated by FOUR waits — 1s, 2s, 4s
# and 8s, 15s of backoff in total. The 30s cap is deliberately out of reach: the delay
# after a fifth failure would be 16s and there is no sixth attempt, so the cap only
# engages if you raise the base delay or the attempt count. Every attempt is logged with its number and error reason, and on
# exhaustion the event is written to its category's <topic>.dlt with failure metadata
# attached.
#
# These names are spelled bare for the same reason as the Kafka block's, and the same
# three forms and precedence apply: BLNK_RELAY_MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS wins, then the bare
# RELAY_MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS below, then BLNK_RELAY_RELAY_MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS.
RELAY_MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS=5
RELAY_RETRY_BASE_BACKOFF_MS=1000
RELAY_RETRY_MAX_BACKOFF_MS=30000

# Subscriber consumer-lag measurement budget
# How many subscribers ONE metrics collection tick examines. Not an R-10 contract
# variable - it accompanies the three above because it is read from the same "relay"
# block and accepts the same three forms with the same precedence.
#
# THIS IS A MONITORING-COVERAGE CONTROL, and the failure it governs is silent. Measuring
# lag costs two broker round trips per authorised topic per tick and leaves a retained
# gauge series per subscriber-topic pair, so the collector stops enumerating the registry
# when this budget is reached. A subscriber past that point is not measured approximately:
# it has NO blnk_kafka_consumer_lag series at all, so SubscriberConsumerLagHigh has nothing
# to evaluate and the subscriber can fall arbitrarily far behind while every dashboard
# reads clean.
#
# SO SIZE IT ABOVE YOUR REGISTRY, with headroom. The shortfall is published as
# blnk_kafka_subscribers_unmeasured{reason="budget"} beside the registry size as
# blnk_subscribers_registered, and SubscriberLagCoverageIncomplete fires on any non-zero
# shortfall - so the gap is visible rather than silent, but visible is not measured.
# WHATEVER YOU SET HERE IS EXPORTED as blnk_subscribers_measurement_budget, so headroom
# is `blnk_subscribers_measurement_budget - blnk_subscribers_registered` and goes negative
# BEFORE the shortfall appears - watch that rather than waiting for the shortfall. Do not
# write the default into a dashboard as a literal: a query like `200 - registered` reads a
# raised budget as exhausted hundreds of subscribers early. A
# budget far above the registry costs nothing, because the sweep stops when the registry
# runs out. Read the reason label before acting: the other four values (unprovisioned,
# measure_failed, registry_failed, topic_missing) are gaps this number cannot close.
#
# 200 is the default. Zero or a negative value is rejected and the default is used: a
# budget of zero would measure nothing at all while reporting no error.
#
# TWO NAMES, ONE KNOB. EVENT_METRICS_SUBSCRIBER_BUDGET sets the same ceiling and is the
# name the alert remediations use. Both are honoured and reconciled onto one value; if
# they disagree, EVENT_METRICS_SUBSCRIBER_BUDGET wins and start-up warns naming both.
RELAY_SUBSCRIBER_METRICS_BUDGET=200

# Event outbox retention (days)
# How long a DISPATCHED event row is kept before it is deleted. Exactly one status is
# age-eligible: dispatched. No other row is ever deleted by this period however old it is,
# which is the TWO POPULATIONS distinction set out below. This is
# a data-protection control, not a storage tuning knob: every row's payload is the
# webhook body verbatim, so a transaction event carries amounts and balance identifiers
# and an identity event carries names, email addresses, phone numbers, postal addresses
# and dates of birth. None of it has operational value once the event is delivered, so
# keeping it forever makes this table an unbounded second copy of the ledger's most
# sensitive data — without the access controls the primary tables have around them.
#
# TWO POPULATIONS, TWO LIFECYCLES. A DISPATCHED row is a receipt — the event reached the
# broker and a subscriber has had it — so age alone governs it. A DEAD-LETTERED row is the
# record of an event NO SUBSCRIBER RECEIVED, and this period does not delete it on its own:
# it becomes eligible only once a REPLAY THE BROKER ACKNOWLEDGES turns it into a dispatched
# receipt, through POST /events/dead-letter/{event_id}/replay. That is the whole of the
# dead-letter workflow — there is no separate "resolve" call, and the route that once
# provided one has been withdrawn because it could not compose with a replay in either
# order (see docs/kafka-operations.md, "Step 5 — There is no resolve step"). An
# unreplayed failure therefore stays however old it is, and keeps the
# DeadLetterMessageStuck alert firing until somebody accounts for it — which is what makes
# any finite value here safe to set.
#
# A pending, processing, replaying or failed row is never eligible however old it is; a
# failed row in particular is excluded because its dead-letter write is still owed, which
# makes the outbox the only copy of that event in existence.
#
# 0 (the default, and the value below) DISABLES retention. That is deliberate: deleting
# ledger-adjacent records is a decision only you can take, because the period your
# jurisdiction, audit programme or any legal hold requires is not something a default
# can guess. Set a positive number of days to switch it on and the sweep then runs hourly
# in the server role, deleting in bounded batches so it never blocks the relay. Rows
# removed are counted by blnk.events.purged.total.
#
# PICK THE NUMBER FROM THE ARITHMETIC, NOT FROM A HABIT (PERF-C03). "30 or 90 days" used
# to be suggested here and both are far too long to set without resizing the database
# first. A row costs 2,372 bytes measured, including all seventeen indexes, of which
# roughly 88% is the event body held three times over — a queryable JSONB projection, the
# byte-exact webhook body, and the byte-exact envelope:
#
#     GiB/day      = rate x 86400 x 2372 / 1024^3
#     steady state = GiB/day x RELAY_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS x 1.5   (bloat/vacuum headroom)
#
# At 500 events a second that is 95.4 GiB A DAY, so 90 days is 12.6 TiB and 3 days is
# 429 GiB. The reference manifests ship 3 days against a 600Gi PostgreSQL volume.
#
# TWO CONSTRAINTS BOUND IT FROM ABOVE. The outbox shares its volume with the LEDGER, so
# an over-generous period exhausts the volume the ledger writes to; and the value must
# stay under the broker's own log.retention.hours, because the daily
# outbox-versus-offset reconciliation can only compare a window both sides still hold.
# The shipped broker retention is 168 hours, so 7 days is the ceiling before the broker
# has to move too.
#
# See docs/kafka-operations.md, "Storage — Size The Volume From The Row".
RELAY_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=0

# Event outbox purge CAPACITY
# How fast the retention period above is enforced, as opposed to how long the period is.
# The two settings multiply, and the sweep runs hourly, so:
#
#     rows deleted per hour = MAX_BATCHES_PER_SWEEP x BATCH_SIZE
#
# COMPARE THAT NUMBER AGAINST YOUR EVENT ARRIVAL RATE before changing either value. If
# capacity is below arrivals the table grows without bound no matter how short the
# retention period is, because the sweeper can never catch up - the period is then simply
# not enforced. At 500 events a second, rows arrive at 1,800,000 an hour; the defaults
# below give 4,000,000 an hour, or 2.2x that. The values in force are logged as
# purge_capacity_rows_per_hour at start-up whenever retention is enabled, and a sweep that
# exhausts its ceiling with rows still eligible logs a warning naming these variables.
#
# THE CEILING WAS 2000 AND IS NOW 4000 (PERF-C03). Two thousand batches gives 1.11x
# arrival, and 11% is not headroom on a mechanism that cannot catch up once it falls
# behind: one sweep cut short by the ten-minute timeout leaves a deficit that takes ten
# hours of steady state to work off. Raising it is free in steady state, because a sweep
# stops as soon as it runs out of ELIGIBLE rows — at peak it still deletes 1,800,000 and
# stops. The ceiling is reached only while catching up.
#
# BATCH_SIZE is the lock-footprint factor: each DELETE takes row locks and writes WAL for
# the rows it removes, and the sweep runs beside a live relay claiming from the same
# table, so keep it small and buy capacity through the batch COUNT instead.
#
# MAX_BATCHES_PER_SWEEP bounds ONE sweep, and the bound is what stops the first sweep
# after you enable retention from attempting an entire historical backlog in a single
# pass. Leave it unset or 0 to take the default. Set it NEGATIVE (-1) to remove the
# ceiling entirely for a deliberate one-off catch-up; such a sweep is still bounded by
# the ten-minute sweep timeout.
RELAY_EVENT_RETENTION_BATCH_SIZE=1000
RELAY_EVENT_RETENTION_MAX_BATCHES_PER_SWEEP=4000

# Relay REPAIR capacity
# How fast the relay clears the two backlogs the ordinary publish claim cannot reach. This
# is a RECOVERY setting, not a throughput one, and the distinction is why it needs its own
# numbers: the publish backlog grows with your ingress rate, these two grow with an OUTAGE.
#
# The two backlogs are:
#
#   * rows whose retry budget is spent and whose <topic>.dlt write also failed. Each is the
#     ONLY copy of an event that reached no topic at all.
#   * rows whose Kafka leg finished and whose legacy webhook enqueue never succeeded, which
#     is the dual-delivery promise for the migration window.
#
# Both are EMPTY in normal operation, so an idle repair pass costs one indexed query
# whatever these values are. They fill all at once: 15 minutes of broker unavailability at
# 500 events a second leaves about 450,000 rows to repair.
#
#     rows per tick = REPAIR_MAX_BATCHES_PER_TICK x REPAIR_BATCH_SIZE
#     drain rate    = rows per tick / poll interval, capped by the broker acknowledgement
#                     time divided by REPAIR_CONCURRENCY
#
# The defaults below give 2,500 rows a tick written 8 at a time, which clears that
# 450,000-row backlog in about ten minutes. Watch two series to know whether they are
# right for your deployment: blnk_events_repair_backlog{leg=...} is what is owed, and
# rate(blnk_events_repair_completed_total{leg=...}[5m]) is how fast it is being paid.
# blnk_events_repair_saturated going to 1 means the per-tick bound — not the backlog — is
# what ended the chain, which is the signal to raise these numbers.
#
# BATCH_SIZE is the lease-footprint factor: every row in a batch needs its own Kafka write
# and the whole batch must finish inside the 30-second claim lease.
#
# MAX_BATCHES_PER_TICK is the throughput factor and the safer of the three to raise. It is
# bounded rather than unbounded so a large repair backlog cannot starve the publish loop it
# shares a tick with — new events keep flowing while the backlog drains.
#
# CONCURRENCY mirrors the publish loop's own width. Ordering is not at risk: rows are
# grouped by the same message key the publisher hashes and each group is written
# sequentially by one worker.
#
# 0 or a negative value in any of the three takes the default. There is deliberately no way
# to switch repair off: a deployment that stopped repairing would silently keep events that
# reached no topic at all.
RELAY_REPAIR_BATCH_SIZE=100
RELAY_REPAIR_MAX_BATCHES_PER_TICK=25
RELAY_REPAIR_CONCURRENCY=8

# Local Kafka stack
# Consumed by docker-compose interpolation for the local single-broker KRaft service and
# by scripts/kafka-provision.sh, following the POSTGRES_IMAGE / POSTGRES_CONTAINER /
# POSTGRES_OUTER_PORT precedent above. BLNK READS NONE OF THE KEYS IN THIS SECTION - they
# configure the local broker and its provisioning, not the application - so none of them
# is part of the R-10 contract described at the top of the Kafka block. The topic geometry
# the broker is provisioned with comes from KAFKA_MIN_PARTITIONS and
# KAFKA_REPLICATION_FACTOR above, which the application and the provisioning script share
# rather than duplicate.
#
# These services only start when the "kafka" compose profile is active - see the note
# on KAFKA_BROKERS above for what activates it.
#
# The image tag has a floor at BOTH ends, and neither is a preference.
#
# LOWER FLOOR - functionality. SASL bootstrap runs "kafka-storage format
# --add-scram", which requires Kafka 3.5 or later (Confluent Platform 7.5.0 or
# later). In KRaft mode SCRAM credentials live in the metadata log and at least one
# user must exist before brokers can authenticate at all, so an earlier image cannot
# be bootstrapped and local bring-up fails outright.
#
# LOWER FLOOR - security. Four advisories bear on this image. Three have ranges firm
# enough to pin against, and the highest of those floors is 4.2.0. Each is stated
# exactly rather than collapsed into "take the newest tag", because they do NOT share
# one floor:
#
#   CVE-2026-33558  affects <=3.9.1 and 4.0.0             fixed 3.9.2, 4.0.1
#     The NetworkClient renders whole requests and responses at DEBUG level, so SASL
#     credentials, SCRAM salts and salted passwords become recoverable from a log file.
#     This is the one that reaches this stack hardest: subscriber provisioning calls
#     AlterUserScramCredentials, which is one of the rendered requests.
#
#   CVE-2026-35554  affects <=3.9.1, <=4.0.1, <=4.1.1     fixed 3.9.2, 4.0.2,
#                                                               4.1.2, 4.2.0
#     A race in the Java producer's buffer pool returns a batch's ByteBuffer to the pool
#     while its request is still in flight, so a later batch for a DIFFERENT topic can
#     reuse it and records are delivered to the wrong topic with no error raised. Blnk
#     publishes through segmentio/kafka-go, so this reaches only the bundled CLI tools -
#     but silent cross-topic delivery would defeat the ACL isolation this broker
#     enforces, so it is treated as a floor rather than as not-applicable.
#
#   CVE-2026-33557  affects 4.1.0-4.1.1                   fixed 4.1.2, 4.2.0
#     The default OAUTHBEARER JWT validator accepts any token without checking
#     signature, issuer or audience. It requires sasl.enabled.mechanisms=OAUTHBEARER,
#     which this stack does not enable - it uses SCRAM-SHA-512 only.
#
#   CVE-2026-41115  range NOT established here            see the CVE list
#     Improper authorization in CONSUMER_GROUP_DESCRIBE's validation of DESCRIBE on the
#     GROUP resource. Named WITHOUT a range on purpose: none was verified, and a floor
#     table with an invented row is worse than one with an honest gap. It bears directly
#     on this stack, because per-subscriber GROUP ACLs are the isolation mechanism.
#
# NO "SAFE UPGRADE TARGET" IS NAMED IN PROSE, deliberately. A version recorded in a
# comment as the safe one is correct on the day it is written and wrong from the next
# advisory onward, and a stale recommendation is read as current: this note previously
# pointed at a 4.0.x release that a later advisory superseded. What is durable is the
# functional floor above, the per-advisory table, the tested pin below, and where to
# look - https://kafka.apache.org/cve-list.
#
# PINNED BY DIGEST, not by tag, and the digest is the part that carries the guarantee.
# A tag is a MUTABLE pointer: the same apache/kafka:4.3.1 can be repushed, so two
# machines pulling this file weeks apart can run different bytes with no diff to show
# for it. A digest is content-addressed, so it cannot. The tag is kept beside it only so
# the release is readable without resolving the hash. It is the SAME digest that
# infrastructure/k8s-manifests/kafka-statefulset.yaml pins, which is what makes local
# bring-up a rehearsal of the deployed broker rather than an approximation of it.
#
# ANY MOVE OFF THIS PIN re-runs three checks, because they are what the stack depends on
# and what a major version can change. All three were re-run for this pin, along with
# the --ignore-formatted idempotency the bootstrap relies on, and 4.3.1 matches 3.9.2 on
# every one: "kafka-storage format --add-scram" seeds the credential (including from an
# argument file), the broker authenticates SCRAM-SHA-512 as uid 1000 under a read-only
# root filesystem, and StandardAuthorizer binds and lists ACLs. Re-formatting an
# already-formatted directory exits 0 WITH --ignore-formatted and 1 without it on BOTH
# releases - that flag is what keeps a restart idempotent in scripts/kafka-bootstrap.sh
# and in the StatefulSet's init container, so do not remove it.
KAFKA_IMAGE=apache/kafka:4.3.1@sha256:77e3df9054047a88b520d0cc46e16696d3b22022e1d580aeccd2632df6532837
KAFKA_CONTAINER=kafka


# Where a GENERATED sample subscriber password is delivered, on the same terms as
# KAFKA_SASL_SECRET_FILE above: mode 0600, path reported, value never printed. With
# neither this nor the secret above set, the provisioning script refuses to run rather
# than print a credential into the kafka-init container log.
#KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_SECRET_FILE=/run/secrets/blnk-kafka-sample-subscriber
KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_SECRET_FILE=

# KAFKA_OUTER_PORT is the HOST port the broker's external listener is published on,
# and it is the only one of the two that was ever configurable. The in-container port
# is fixed at 9092 by the listener configuration in docker-compose.yaml, where
# BROKER://kafka:9092 is what every other container dials; a KAFKA_INNER_PORT variable
# used to be listed here and was read by nothing, so setting it changed no port and
# silently promised something the stack does not do.
KAFKA_OUTER_PORT=9092

# KAFKA IS OPT-IN. The kafka and kafka-init services sit behind the "kafka" Compose
# profile, so "docker compose up" starts neither — which is what lets the documented
# no-broker steady state above actually start. Selecting the profile is what brings up
# the broker and provisions the topic catalogue and the two principals.
#
#   docker compose up                          -> no Kafka at all
#   docker compose --profile kafka up           -> broker + provisioning
#   COMPOSE_PROFILES=kafka  (set below)         -> the same, without the flag
#
# OPTING IN HAS TWO HALVES AND YOU NEED BOTH: select the profile so there is a broker,
# and set KAFKA_BROKERS=kafka:9092 so Blnk publishes to it. The profile alone gives a
# provisioned broker Blnk ignores; KAFKA_BROKERS alone gives a relay retrying against
# nothing. Add "monitoring" alongside it — COMPOSE_PROFILES=kafka,monitoring — to bring
# up Prometheus and all 13 Kafka alert rules as well.
#COMPOSE_PROFILES=kafka
COMPOSE_PROFILES=
# The host interface the broker's SASL listener is published on. LEAVE IT AT LOOPBACK.
#
# That listener speaks SASL_PLAINTEXT, so the SCRAM exchange and every ledger event on
# it travel unencrypted. Published on 0.0.0.0 - which is what compose does when no host
# address is given - it is reachable from anywhere that can route to this machine, which
# puts financial amounts and identity records on the wire in cleartext for anyone on the
# path AND exposes the broker superuser handshake. Everything that uses this port is
# local: host-run tests, a host-run "blnk start", and the k6 event scenario. Change it
# only together with a plan for encrypting the listener.
KAFKA_OUTER_HOST=127.0.0.1


# Advanced local-broker knobs, listed because the compose files interpolate them and an
# operator cannot otherwise discover that they exist. Every one carries the default shown, so
# leaving them commented changes nothing; they are here to be found, not to be set.
#
# KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL selects the listener the broker publishes and the protocol every
# client speaks to it. SASL_PLAINTEXT is authenticated but NOT encrypted, which is why the
# application additionally requires KAFKA_INSECURE_LOCAL_DEV to be acknowledged above. Use
# SASL_SSL with KAFKA_TLS_ENABLED=true for anything reachable beyond this machine.
#KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL=SASL_PLAINTEXT

# KAFKA_SCRAM_ITERATIONS is the PBKDF2 iteration count every SCRAM credential this stack
# creates is derived with. Kafka's floor is 4096 and both scripts refuse anything lower.
# Raising it costs a little CPU per authentication and nothing else.
#KAFKA_SCRAM_ITERATIONS=4096

# KAFKA_CLUSTER_ID pins the KRaft cluster ID the broker's storage is formatted with. Unset
# means one is generated on the first format and then read back from the volume's own
# meta.properties on every later start. Set it only to reproduce a specific cluster identity;
# a value that disagrees with an already-formatted volume makes formatting fail outright.
#KAFKA_CLUSTER_ID=

# The sample subscriber scripts/kafka-provision.sh creates so a local consumer has an
# identity to use. It is granted Read and Describe on the four grantable DOMAIN category
# topics (transactions, balances, identities, ledgers) and Read on its own consumer-group
# prefix - never Write, never a .dlt topic and never blnk.system, which this script grants
# to nobody. Narrow it with KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_TOPICS when you want a topic outside the
# grant to prove a denial on. Leave the secret empty and one is generated on the first run, written
# to a mode-0600 file and preserved on later runs so a routine bring-up cannot invalidate the
# credential a consumer is already holding. Set KAFKA_SKIP_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER=1 on a shared or
# production broker, where subscriber principals belong to
# POST /subscribers/{id}/kafka-credentials rather than to a script.
#
# The consumer-group namespace is not listed: it is DERIVED from the principal plus the
# group terminator, and kafka-provision.sh refuses any explicit value that differs. Even
# commented out, the unterminated spelling was a trap — uncommenting it is exactly the
# edit that made provisioning refuse to run.
#KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_USER=blnk-sample-subscriber
#KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_SECRET=
# Its consumer-group namespace is derived, not configured - see the note above the active
# KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_GROUP_PREFIX line. Leave it empty unless you want the cross-check.
#KAFKA_SAMPLE_SUBSCRIBER_GROUP_PREFIX=

KAFKA_OUTER_ADVERTISED_HOST=localhost


# Observability — the switch that makes /metrics EXIST
# It is not a verbosity setting. Each role registers its metrics endpoint only when the
# OpenTelemetry SDK was set up, and the SDK is set up only when this is true:
#
#   cmd/server.go   initializeRouter          -> if h := trace.MetricsHandler(); h != nil
#   cmd/workers.go  monitoring server (:5004) -> the same guard
#
# With it false there is NO /metrics route on either role. Every scrape returns 404, no
# series is ever recorded, and the 13 rules in alerts/blnk-kafka-alerts.yml cannot
# fire — a state that reports itself nowhere: Prometheus shows the target down, the rules
# report no error, and the deployment looks monitored.
#
# BOTH COMPOSE FILES NOW DEFAULT IT TO TRUE, so `docker compose --profile monitoring up`
# works as it reads. Before that, selecting the monitoring profile started Prometheus,
# mounted the rules, scraped both roles and got 404 from both — arming observability took
# an edit nothing documented. Compose cannot make an environment value depend on a
# selected profile, so it is armed unconditionally instead: the cost when nobody scrapes
# is one in-process registry, and the cost of the other default is every alert, silently.
#
# Set it to false to opt out. In Kubernetes the same value ships as
# BLNK_ENABLE_OBSERVABILITY in infrastructure/k8s-manifests/blnk-config.yaml.
#
# BLNK_ENABLE_OBSERVABILITY=true

# Prometheus scrape authentication
# Blnk's /metrics endpoint is guarded by MetricsAuthHandler, and its behaviour is what
# decides whether ANY of the Kafka alerts in alerts/blnk-kafka-alerts.yml can ever fire.
#
# LEAVE THIS UNSET for the local monitoring profile and scrapes succeed
# unauthenticated, which is why prometheus.yml ships with its authorization blocks
# commented out.
#
# SET IT and every request without "Authorization: Bearer <token>" is refused — which
# means the scrape jobs collect NOTHING, every series the alert rules match on is
# absent, and every alert sits permanently unable to fire. That failure is silent:
# Prometheus reports the target as down, the rules report no error, and an alert that
# cannot fire is indistinguishable from a system with nothing wrong. So if you set
# this, follow the two enabling steps documented in prometheus.yml — the token file
# mount plus uncommenting the authorization blocks — and then confirm at
# http://localhost:9090 that /targets shows both jobs UP *and* that /rules lists all
# 13 rules in the blnk-kafka-alerts group:
#
#   DeadLetterMessageStuck
#   SubscriberConsumerLagHigh
#   SubscriberRevocationOutstanding
#   SubscriberCredentialOrphaned
#   SubscriberRevocationRefused
#   ConsumerLagMeasurementDegraded
#   SubscriberLagCoverageStale
#   SubscriberLagCoverageIncomplete
#   SubscriberSettlementNotProgressing
#   SubscriberSettlementOutstanding
#   EventMetricsCollectionStale
#   EventMetricsCollectionFailing
#   EventMetricsCollectionAbsent
#
# EventMetricsCollectionAbsent is the one to look for first. It fires precisely when no
# collector is reporting, which is the state an unauthenticated-but-token-guarded scrape
# produces — so it is the rule that turns this silent failure into a page instead of
# leaving every other rule evaluating a series that does not exist.
#
# One token guards both the server and the worker endpoints. In Kubernetes it is not
# optional: infrastructure/k8s-manifests/prometheus-configmap.yaml authenticates
# unconditionally and reads the value from Secret blnk-metrics-token, key
# metrics-bearer-token — and both Deployments read that SAME Secret and key, so one
# `kubectl create secret` serves the scraper and the scraped rather than two values that
# have to be kept equal.
#
# BLNK_METRICS_BEARER_TOKEN=
